    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im studying for sec+ and im kinda stuck on something. if you hash a password it's already encrypted/scrambled right? so why do we even bother with the \"salt\" part? doesnt the hash already make it unique?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While a hash produces a unique fingerprint of an input, it is deterministic, meaning the same password always produces the same hash. A salt is added to the password *before* hashing, ensuring that two identical passwords result in completely different hashes. This prevents attackers from using rainbow tables, which are pre-computed lists of hashes for common passwords.",
