    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i think i get the deterministic part. but how do the rainbow tables actually work? like, if i use a salt, does that just make the rainbow table too big to use or something? how does the math play into it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Rainbow tables are large, pre-computed databases that store hashes for vast numbers of common inputs and their corresponding original values. They work by creating mathematical chains to quickly reverse a hash without trying every possibility. Adding a salt essentially creates a unique input for every salt value, forcing the attacker to build a separate, massive rainbow table for every single salt used, making the attack computationally infeasible.",
